<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    CircleAI Service — the installed app that OWNS the models.

    THE SAME SHAPE AS AetherNetService, deliberately. That pair already settled how a
    service and a thin app live together on one device:

      src/AetherNetService          the service APK, its own ApplicationId, runs the radios
      samples/.../AetherNet.Sample  the thin app - "runs no radios ... takes its identity
                                    from the separate AetherNetService app"
      src/AetherNet.Node.Android    the client library BOTH of them reference

    CircleAI maps onto it one for one: this project, CircleAI.Samples.App, and
    CircleAI.Client. Aether runs no radios; the thin CircleAI app runs no model, no
    recogniser, no voice and no microphone. The hardware and the megabytes live here.

    WHY IT EXISTS AS ITS OWN PACKAGE. A 122 MB voice takes 13-23 s to build a session
    on a P30 Lite, and fifteen apps on a 3 GB phone cannot each hold their own copy.
    A person who wants CircleAI on their phone installs CircleAI, not a sample of it.

    IT SHIPS NO SCREENS AND HAS NO LAUNCHER ENTRY. A service does not have a UI. It
    had one - StatusActivity, [Activity(MainLauncher = true)] - and that was wrong:
    it put this app in the launcher as something a person opens, which is exactly the
    confusion the split exists to remove. Two store listings; only one is an app you
    open.

    THE ONE ACTIVITY IS NOT AN EXCEPTION. LinkConsentActivity (in CircleAI.Device) is
    translucent, ExcludeFromRecents, has no launcher filter, and is launched FOR
    RESULT by a client - because a background service cannot raise a biometric sheet,
    and starting it for result is how the OS tells the consent screen which package is
    really asking. It is the client's approval dialog wearing this process.

    SO HOW DOES IT START? A client binds to CircleNeuronLinkService, Android starts
    the process, and Application.OnCreate wires everything before the bind is
    answered. That is the whole entry point.

    THE MANIFEST IS GENERATED FROM ATTRIBUTES in CircleAI.Device, which is why no XML
    here declares anything:
      CircleNeuronLinkService  [Service(Exported = true)] + [IntentFilter(BindAction)]
      LinkConsentActivity      [Activity(Exported = true)] + [IntentFilter(ConsentAction)]
      CircleNeuronService      [Service(ForegroundServiceType = DataSync | Microphone)]
    Referencing CircleAI.Device is what puts them in the package.

    ANDROID TODAY; iOS AND WINDOWS ARE THE POINT OF THE SHAPE. Everything platform-
    specific is behind CircleAI.Device and CircleAI.Assistant.Device, so a second head
    is another TargetFramework and its own host class, not a second product.
    AetherNetService is Android-only today for the same reason and by the same plan.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-android;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>24</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>

    <RootNamespace>CircleAI.Service.Android</RootNamespace>

    <!-- MUST match LinkIpc.HostPackage, or every client's IsInstalled() reads false
         and no bind is ever attempted. The two are one fact; the constant is the one
         place it is written down. -->
    <ApplicationId>com.bhengubv.circleai.service</ApplicationId>

    <!-- Android's versionCode. It MUST increase on every build somebody could install
         over, or the install is refused with no useful message. -->
    <ApplicationVersion>36</ApplicationVersion>
    <ApplicationDisplayVersion>0.1</ApplicationDisplayVersion>

    <!-- Plain APK so `adb install` works without a bundletool step. Also: a Release
         -t:Install of an app bundle UNINSTALLS first and takes the downloaded models
         with it, which on a P30 is gigabytes. -->
    <AndroidPackageFormat>apk</AndroidPackageFormat>

    <!-- The permissions live here; the components are generated from attributes in
         CircleAI.Device. Same arrangement as AetherNetService. -->
    <AndroidManifest>Platforms\Android\AndroidManifest.xml</AndroidManifest>

    <!-- The linked Android sources are guarded by this, as in every head that compiles
         them - without it AndroidAudio.cs compiles to an empty file and the type reads
         as a missing namespace rather than a missing definition. -->
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);IT_VOICE_ANDROID</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The resident service, the link service and the consent activity all live here.
         This single reference is what makes this app the host. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CircleAI.Device\CircleAI.Device.csproj" Condition="$(TargetFramework.Contains('-android'))" />

    <!-- The engines. This app owns model selection and loading, so it is the one
         package that needs them; the client has no inference dependency at all, which
         is the entire point of the split. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CircleAI.Inference\CircleAI.Inference.csproj" />

    <!-- The assistant's device half: the microphone and speaker, the recogniser and
         the voice (ServiceSpeech), the model store, the skill library, VoiceWiring. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CircleAI.Assistant.Device\CircleAI.Assistant.Device.csproj" Condition="$(TargetFramework.Contains('-android'))" />

    <!-- The client library, referenced by BOTH halves - exactly as AetherNetService
         references AetherNet.Node.Android. It carries AppPaths and SqliteAppStore,
         which both processes need to find their own storage. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CircleAI.Client\CircleAI.Client.csproj" Condition="$(TargetFramework.Contains('-android'))" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      espeak-ng AND THE SKILL DATABASE. Both were in the thin app and neither could be
      used there: espeak-ng is a grapheme-to-phoneme front end for SYNTHESIS, and
      skills.db is what a MODEL looks things up in. This is the process with both.

      arm64 only for the .so - the x86_64 build would need the NDK recipe run again -
      and the library and its data are version-locked at espeak-ng 1.52.0, so they move
      together or not at all.

      espeak-ng is GPL-3.0 and is used OUT OF PROCESS, never linked. That arrangement
      is what keeps it off this app's licence, and it is why the phonemiser is invoked
      rather than referenced.
    -->
    <AndroidNativeLibrary Include="..\CircleAI.Voice\runtimes\android-arm64\native\libespeak-ng.so"
                          Abi="arm64-v8a" />
    <AndroidAsset Include="..\CircleAI.Voice\assets\espeak-ng-data.zip"
                  Link="espeak-ng-data.zip" />
    <AndroidAsset Include="..\CircleAI.Skills\assets\skills.db.zip"
                  Link="skills.db.zip" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    MNN's native libraries are bundled by the repo-root Directory.Build.targets; no
    per-project plumbing. This app does NOT opt out - it is the one that loads them.

    The llama.cpp bridge is not wired in here yet. libllamabridge.so is built (arm64,
    against PrismML-Eng/llama.cpp prism-b10743-adfffbe) and needs four companion
    libraries - libllama, libggml, libggml-cpu, libggml-base, about 61 MB together.
    Adding them is a deliberate step with a real size cost.
  -->

  <!--
    THE TWO HEADS OF ONE SERVICE.

    net10.0-android is the bound service: a binder other apps attach to by package
    name, a foreground notification, the microphone. net10.0 is the same engine on a
    desktop, where that shape has no equivalent - so the transport becomes the
    loopback HTTP endpoint CircleAI.Hosting already carries and the rest is unchanged.

    ONE PROJECT RATHER THAN TWO, because it is one service with two front doors; a
    second project would be a second place to forget to bump a version or wire a
    dependency. The platform split is by file, not by repository.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="!$(TargetFramework.Contains('-android'))">
    <!-- The Android head's Application class, its manifest and its launcher icon are
         meaningless off the phone, and Resources/** would be fed to a resource
         compiler that is not there. -->
    <Compile Remove="ServiceApplication.cs" />
    <None Remove="Resources\**" />
    <None Remove="Platforms\**" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="$(TargetFramework.Contains('-android'))">
    <!-- And the desktop entry point is not an Android app's; Android has no Main. -->
    <Compile Remove="Host\**" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- BOTH HEADS. The desktop one answers on HttpLoopbackEndpoint, which lives
         here; the Android one names AIOptions directly when it sets
         CircleNeuronService.OptionsFactory. That reference used to arrive
         transitively through CircleAI.Device, so conditioning this to the desktop
         leg broke the Android build with CS0012 - a type "defined in an assembly
         that is not referenced", which reads as a missing package and is a missing
         transitive. Named explicitly, for both. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CircleAI.Hosting\CircleAI.Hosting.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
